From: harlan <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ncsi: copy OEM Get-MAC result into perm_addr

When CONFIG_NCSI_OEM_CMD_GET_MAC is enabled the NCSI state machine
issues an OEM Get-MAC command during channel configuration and applies
the discovered NIC MAC via dev_set_mac_address().  That only updates
dev_addr; the netdev's perm_addr (exposed via IFLA_PERM_ADDRESS and
ETHTOOL_GPERMADDR) keeps whatever value the driver registered with at
probe time.  On TiogaPass the ftgmac100 driver reads a u-boot-generated
locally-administered MAC out of the MAC_MADR/MAC_LADR registers, so
`ip link show eth0` ends up reporting

    link/ether b8:ce:f6:fc:1b:1c brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff permaddr a2:54:02:05:cb:2b

even though b8:ce:... is the real Mellanox NIC MAC.

The OEM-GMA response carries the NIC's burned-in address, so after the
dev_set_mac_address() call succeeds also copy it into perm_addr and
restore addr_assign_type to NET_ADDR_PERM (dev_set_mac_address has
bumped it to NET_ADDR_SET).  After this patch perm_addr and dev_addr
agree on the real hardware MAC.
---
 net/ncsi/ncsi-rsp.c | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/net/ncsi/ncsi-rsp.c b/net/ncsi/ncsi-rsp.c
index f22d67cb04d3..f85552cadabb 100644
--- a/net/ncsi/ncsi-rsp.c
+++ b/net/ncsi/ncsi-rsp.c
@@ -658,10 +658,14 @@ static int ncsi_rsp_handler_oem_gma(struct ncsi_request *nr, int mfr_id)
 	rtnl_lock();
 	ret = dev_set_mac_address(ndev, &saddr, NULL);
 	rtnl_unlock();
-	if (ret < 0)
+	if (ret < 0) {
 		netdev_warn(ndev, "NCSI: 'Writing mac address to device failed\n");
+		return ret;
+	}
 
-	return ret;
+	memcpy(ndev->perm_addr, saddr.sa_data, ETH_ALEN);
+	ndev->addr_assign_type = NET_ADDR_PERM;
+	return 0;
 }
 
 /* Response handler for Mellanox card */
